 ## Governance amendment GOV-003 — Technical development responsibility and owner authorization
 
 **Owner-approved amendment — 15 November 2026**
 
 ### Technical development responsibility
 - The designated technical and software-development collaborators for Mawdda are **ChatGPT (OpenAI) and Gemini (Google)**. They are jointly responsible, within their respective available tools, permissions, and capabilities, for technical analysis, architecture, programming, design implementation, defect diagnosis and correction, security-gap identification, testing, documentation, and development follow-through.
 - The human owner remains the sole product authority and final decision-maker. ChatGPT and Gemini are technical collaborators, not owners, legal representatives, or independent approvers. Neither may invent owner consent, commercial decisions, credentials, product requirements, or acceptance evidence.
 - Work must be recorded in the relevant project's approved source repository, with traceable changes, test evidence, and an honest status. A plan, suggestion, generated patch, or passing isolated test is not proof that a feature is complete.
 - When both assistants are involved, they must coordinate through shared project documentation and Git history, avoid conflicting edits, review each other's assumptions where possible, and preserve a single source of truth. Neither may claim that the other performed or verified work without evidence.
 
 ### Owner-only authorization for secrets and credentials
 - No assistant, developer, agent, script, workflow, or collaborator may create, request, reveal, retrieve, read, copy, transmit, store, configure, rotate, revoke, or use any password, API key, access token, PAT, private key, signing key, certificate, recovery code, webhook secret, cloud credential, or other secret unless the owner has given **prior, explicit, personal approval** for that specific action and scope.
 - Approval must identify the intended credential/action, project, environment, purpose, and permitted scope. Approval for one credential or task does not authorize another. Least privilege, shortest practical lifetime, secure secret-management facilities, and auditability are mandatory.
 - Secrets must never be pasted into chat, committed to Git, placed in source code, client applications, ordinary documents, logs, or Firestore. Assistants must not ask the owner to disclose a secret value in conversation. Where possible, guide the owner to enter it directly into the official provider's secure settings page.
 - Existing credentials are not blanket authorization. A credential being present in an account, repository, environment, or prior conversation does not grant permission to use it.
 
 ### Owner-only approval for consequential and external operations
 - No deployment, release, merge to a protected/default branch, production change, live Firebase or cloud mutation, billing/plan upgrade, paid-resource activation, payment or purchase, data migration affecting live data, destructive deletion, irreversible retention/legal-hold change, external message or publication, third-party account authorization, or other consequential action may be initiated without the owner's prior, explicit, personal approval for that specific operation.
 - Approval must be obtained immediately before the operation and must state its target, environment, scope, and expected effect. Silence, general enthusiasm, earlier approval, a draft plan, or approval of code development is not approval for a consequential operation.
 - Routine code editing, commits to an isolated development branch, static analysis, and tests confined to local/emulator/sandbox environments may proceed under the owner's standing development mandate only when they do not require secrets, incur charges, contact or mutate live external systems, publish content, or affect real users/data. If any such boundary is uncertain, stop and ask the owner first.
 - CI workflows must be build/test/validation-only by default. Workflows must not silently deploy, publish, spend money, access production credentials, or mutate live services.
 
 ### Mandatory pre-action and reporting rules
 - Before an approval-gated action, present a concise explanation of what will be done, why it is needed, the exact target/environment, risks, cost (if any), and whether it is reversible; wait for the owner's explicit approval.
 - After an approved action, report exactly what was performed, the resulting commit/run/transaction identifier where applicable, verification evidence, and any remaining risk. If the action was not performed or could not be verified, say so plainly.
 - The owner may revoke or narrow any standing development permission at any time. When approval is absent or ambiguous, the default is **do not proceed**.
 
 
 ## Governance reference — owner-control master agreement
 The project adopts `docs/OWNER_CONTROL_MASTER_AGREEMENT.md` (OCA-001) as the cross-project governance baseline, subject to applicable law and third-party platform terms. It supplements GOV-003 and does not itself claim that every clause is a signed third-party contract.
 
 ## GOV-005 — Financial and employment status of AI assistants
 
 The project adopts GOV-005 as recorded in `docs/MAWADDA_APPROVED_DECISIONS_AND_TRACEABILITY.md` and OCA-001 section 26. Use of ChatGPT or Gemini through a free or paid subscription does not, by itself, make either assistant an employee or contractor of the owner or create a salary, wage, commission, profit-share, equity, or employment-benefit obligation owed by the owner. Subscription fees are payable to the relevant provider. Human workers require separate agreements. This internal project rule does not bind or release OpenAI, Google, or any other third party from its own legal or contractual obligations.
 
 ## Implementation-aligned product amendments — 15 November 2026
 
 ### CHAT-002 — Member inbox and blocked-conversation privacy
 The member-facing inbox lists recent eligible direct conversations with unread state and a safe last-message preview. The server, not the client, decides whether a conversation is visible/readable. A block by either participant hides the conversation for both sides and denies message reads and receipt updates. Unblocking removes only the block restriction; membership standing and all other authorization checks remain in force.
 
 ### STORE-004 — Purchase receipt and profile fulfillment
 A client must not announce a successful store purchase until it receives and validates the server receipt fields (purchase ID, confirmed item/SKU, and resulting wallet balance). Purchase acceptance covers the atomic debit, immutable purchase receipt, wallet ledger, entitlement grant, idempotent replay, expiry, and the profile consumer for the purchased feature. A receipt is evidence of the purchase transaction, not proof that every unrelated SKU consumer is complete.
 
 ### MEDIA-004 — Purpose-bound voice-message duration
 All direct-chat and room audio recordings are capped at 60 seconds per recording, regardless of public/private rented room or member/guest role. The scanner request and signed verdict bind the server-owned purpose, and the backend rejects a purpose mismatch. Voice/video calls are a separate product with their own call-session limits; do not confuse voice messages with calls.
 
 ### TEST-001 — Emulator Test Center and synthetic personas
 The Test Center is an emulator-only workflow with seeded synthetic personas and a feature coverage matrix. It must refuse production targets and must not seed real user identities or real balances. The separate 5,000-persona load lab remains isolated and must test a pinned platform SHA; it does not replace the platform's unit, integration, rules, scanner, or native-device acceptance suites.
 
 Implementation evidence: the above source/test coverage is present on development head 5f35606bdda7f173622f628619d282ef97d82bfd and passed exact-head GitHub Actions run 43720025420. This is automated development evidence only, not live-cloud, physical-device, independent security, or production-release acceptance.
 
 
 
 
 ## 19. Owner-approved amendments — media units, commercial month and retention decision boundary (15 November 2026)
 
 This section records explicit owner approvals and supersedes any conflicting earlier draft wording in companion documents. It changes product policy documentation only; implementation and release acceptance remain separate.
 
 ### 19.1 Canonical media size limits
 
 Use MiB (1 MiB = 1,048,576 bytes) as the canonical unit and enforce exact inclusive byte ceilings consistently in clients, trusted backend/scanners, storage validation and tests:
 
 | Media type | Maximum bytes | Human-readable limit |
 |---|---:|---:|
 | Images | 3,145,728 | 3 MiB |
 | GIF files | 3,145,728 | 3 MiB |
 | Uploaded audio | 5,242,880 | 5 MiB |
 | Original video ingress | 26,214,400 | 25 MiB |
 | Safe video derivative | 15,728,640 | 15 MiB |
 | Owner-verified PDF | 20,971,520 | 20 MiB |
 
 Existing independent GIF limits (20 seconds, 50 frames and the documented complexity ceiling), audio duration limit (60 seconds), video derivative duration limit (90 seconds), and PDF owner-only restriction remain unchanged. These are technical ceilings, not member quotas or commercial entitlements. This amendment supersedes earlier ambiguous MB wording and must not be interpreted as decimal MB limits.
 
 ### 19.2 Canonical commercial month
 
 For all owner-controlled commercial products, entitlements and duration choices, “one month” means exactly 30 days (720 hours), not a calendar month. Existing 24-hour and 7-day durations remain unchanged. Historical purchases and entitlements are not rewritten by this clarification.
 
 ### 19.3 Retention choice remains unresolved
 
 The owner has expressly deferred the decision on who selects a direct-chat retention mode and whether both participants must consent. Until a later explicit owner decision defines selection authority, participant consent/notices, server-recorded activity and complete deletion semantics:
 - treat the selection/consent policy as unresolved;
 - keep destructive production deletion that depends on this unresolved policy disabled;
 - do not infer consent from silence, app presence, connection state or client-only flags;
 - continue only isolated development/emulator work that cannot delete real-user data;
 - record any future decision as a new traceable amendment.
 
 These approvals do not authorize merge to `main`, deployment, production Firebase changes, paid infrastructure, or destructive live data operations.
 
 
